      "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nLet $Y=2-\\frac{X}{5}.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $a$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $b$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $c$ | $d$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=2-\\frac{X}{5}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSo, we have $a=1,b=-2,c=\\frac{1}{4},$ and therefore $a+b+c=-\\frac{3}{4}.$",
